hili LLM", "cross-border data transfer AI", "MCP healthcare", "fractional CTO Africa", "clinical AI safety"], "faq": [["Does Kenya regulate AI in healthcare?", "Kenya has no AI-specific law in force. The Data Protection Act 2019 applies, with health data treated as sensitive personal data. The ODPC has consulted on draft guidance on AI, and commentary on that draft says health diagnostics and clinical decision support would need a data protection impact assessment."], ["Can a Kenyan provider send patient data to an offshore AI model?", "Cross-border transfers are allowed only with appropriate safeguards under the Data Protection Act, and there is growing public pressure for stronger protections when data goes to offshore AI providers. Minimising and de-identifying data before it leaves the country reduces both legal and practical risk."], ["Should an AI assistant make triage decisions on its own?", "Not in this design. Rules catch red-flag symptoms first, the model drafts a summary and suggested routing, and a clinician confirms before it affects care."]], "related": ["/healthcare-data-architecture/", "/ai-architect/", "/regions/"], "citations": [{"title": "The Data Protection Act, No. 24 of 2019", "url": "http://kenyalaw.org/kl/fileadmin/pdfdocs/Acts/2019/TheDataProtectionAct__No24of2019.pdf", "publisher": "Kenya Law", "date": "2019"}, {"title": "Draft ODPC Guidance Notes on AI, Emerging Technology and Privacy Enhancing Technologies", "url": "https://posts.kictanet.or.ke/draft-odpc-guidance-notes-on-ai-emerging-technology-and-privacy-enhancing-technologies/", "publisher": "KICTANet", "date": "2026"}, {"title": "Kenya forces impact assessments on recommendation engines and 7 other AI uses", "url": "https://ppc.land/kenya-forces-impact-assessments-on-recommendation-engines-and-7-other-ai-uses/", "publisher": "PPC Land", "date": "2026"}, {"title": "Kenya Wants Stronger Privacy Protections for Data Sent to Offshore AI Providers", "url": "https://techtrendske.co.ke/2026/08/06/kenya-offshore-ai-data-protection/", "publisher": "TechTrends KE", "date": "6 Aug 2026"}, {"title": "African regulators lean on data protection laws as dedicated AI legislation stalls, report finds", "url": "https://www.business-humanrights.org/en/latest-news/african-regulators-lean-on-data-protection-laws-as-dedicated-ai-legislation-stalls-report-finds/", "publisher": "Business and Human Rights Resource Centre", "date": "Oct 2026"}, {"title": "What is the Model Context Protocol (MCP)?", "url": "https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/getting-started/intro", "publisher": "Model Context Protocol", "date": "2026"}]}-->
<h2>The situation</h2>
<p>A telehealth provider in Kenya offered consultations by app, WhatsApp and SMS, backed by a network of clinicians and partner pharmacies. Demand peaked in the evenings, when patients waited longest for a first response. The team wanted an AI assistant to take a structured symptom history in English or Swahili, summarise it and suggest who should see the patient, so clinicians could start from a clear picture.</p>
<p><strong>Why now.</strong> Kenya's Data Protection Act treats health data as sensitive personal data and allows transfers abroad only with appropriate safeguards<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. The Office of the Data Protection Commissioner (ODPC) consulted in 2026 on draft guidance on AI<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>, and commentary on the draft says uses such as health diagnostics and clinical decision support would need a data protection impact assessment<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. There were also public calls for stronger protection of data sent to offshore AI providers<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>, and an October 2026 report described African regulators, Kenya's among them, using data protection law to police AI while dedicated AI bills stall<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. A dedicated AI law may or may not arrive soon. The data protection duties already apply.</p>

<h2>What had to be true</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Safety first</strong>: urgent symptoms escalated by rules, never left to a model's judgement.</li>
<li><strong>Clinician in the loop</strong>: the AI drafts, a clinician decides.</li>
<li><strong>Identifiable data stays home</strong>: names, numbers and IDs never reach an offshore model.</li>
<li><strong>Both languages work</strong>, including mixed English and Swahili, tested on every change.</li>
<li><strong>A living DPIA</strong>, updated with each release and backed by evidence.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Architecture</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Channels</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>App<em>rich chat</em></span><span>WhatsApp<em>most patients</em></span><span>SMS<em>basic phones</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Safety</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Red-flag rules<em>run before the model</em></span><span>Emergency routing<em>call-back and referral</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>In-country boundary</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">De-identification gateway<em>strip and tokenise identifiers</em></span><span>MCP servers<em>clinic, pharmacy, scheduling</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Model</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Hosted LLM<em>zero retention, de-identified text</em></span><span>Prompt and policy pack<em>versioned</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Care</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Clinician review queue<em>summary, suggestion, full history</em></span><span>Records<em>in-country EHR</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Evidence</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Traces<em>no raw content</em></span><span>Evaluations<em>English, Swahili, mixed</em></span><span>DPIA<em>per release</em></span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Rules guard safety, the gateway guards identity, and clinicians guard decisions.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>The data flow, end to end</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<ol class="dgm-flow g3">
<li><b>Message</b><span>A patient describes symptoms by app, WhatsApp or SMS</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Screen</b><span>Rules check for red flags; urgent cases go straight to a clinician</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>De-identify</b><span>Names, numbers and IDs are replaced with tokens in-country</span></li>
<li><b>Draft</b><span>The model asks follow-up questions and drafts a summary and routing</span></li>
<li><b>Review</b><span>A clinician sees the summary, the suggestion and the full history</span></li>
<li><b>Record</b><span>The decision is stored in the in-country record with the model version</span></li>
</ol>
<figcaption>Nothing the model says affects care until a clinician has confirmed it.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>The hard part: giving the model context without giving it identities</h2>
<p>Good triage needs context: age band, past visits, current medicines, allergies. Shipping the whole record to the model would have been easy and wrong. The team instead exposed clinic systems through small MCP servers running in-country, each offering narrow, read-only tools such as "medicines for this patient token" or "visits in the last 90 days"<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>. The servers return only the fields the tool needs, keyed by token, so the model can reason about the patient without ever seeing who they are.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/kenya-telehealth-ai-triage-data-protection/mcp-overview.webp" alt="Diagram of the Model Context Protocol connecting AI applications such as chat interfaces, IDEs and other AI apps with data sources and tools such as databases, development tools and productivity tools through bidirectional data flow" width="1400" height="547" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>MCP sits between the assistant and clinic systems, so each tool can decide exactly what leaves the boundary. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/getting-started/intro" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Model Context Protocol docs, MCP contributors</a> · CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure>
<p>De-identification in free text is harder than in records. Patients type names, phone numbers, locations and the names of relatives, often mixing English and Swahili. The gateway combined pattern rules with a small local model for entity detection, tested on real, consented examples in both languages, and failed closed: if it was unsure, the message went to a human rather than to the model. More on securing tool servers in the <a href="/insights/securing-mcp-servers-checklist/">MCP security checklist</a>.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>The question is not "can we send this to the model?" but "what is the least the model needs to be useful?" Build tools that answer exactly that.</p></div>

<h2>How it ran</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-lanes">
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Clinical lead</small><ol><li>Red-flag rules</li><li class="hl">Review workflow</li><li>Safety reviews</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Engineering</small><ol><li>Gateway and MCP servers</li><li class="hl">Evaluation harness</li><li>Tracing</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Data protection</small><ol><li>DPIA</li><li>Transfer safeguards</li><li class="hl">Patient notices</li></ol></div>
</div>
<figcaption>The clinical lead owned safety; engineering owned the boundary; data protection owned the paper trail.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-phases">
<div><small>Phase 1</small><b>Safety and DPIA</b><ul><li>Red-flag rules</li><li>Draft DPIA</li><li>Clinician workflow</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 2</small><b>Boundary</b><ul><li>De-identification gateway</li><li>MCP servers</li><li>Zero-retention model</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 3</small><b>Shadow mode</b><ul><li>AI drafts unseen</li><li>Compare with clinicians</li><li>Fix gaps</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 4</small><b>Assist</b><ul><li>Drafts shown to clinicians</li><li>Evening pilot</li><li>DPIA updated</li></ul></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Shadow mode first: the AI's drafts were compared with clinicians' own triage before anyone saw them.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>Key decisions and trade-offs</h2>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Alternative</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Rules before the model for red flags</td><td>Let the model spot emergencies</td><td>Predictable, testable and explainable</td></tr>
<tr><td>De-identify in-country</td><td>Send records under a contract</td><td>Identifiable health data never leaves</td></tr>
<tr><td>Narrow MCP tools</td><td>Give the model the whole record</td><td>Least data for each question</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fail closed on uncertain redaction</td><td>Send and hope</td><td>A human is cheaper than a breach</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shadow mode before assist</td><td>Pilot directly with patients</td><td>Find errors without risk to care</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>What I'd do again, and differently</h2>
<p>Again: shadow mode. It showed where the model asked poor follow-up questions in Swahili before any patient saw them. Differently: build the bilingual evaluation set first, not alongside the gateway. It was the slowest part to get right and it drove most design choices. Related: <a href="/case-studies/ambient-clinical-documentation-private-ai/">private AI for clinical documentation</a>, <a href="/insights/private-llm-on-prem/">private LLMs</a>, <a href="/insights/llm-evaluation-sets-that-predict-production/">evaluation sets</a>, <a href="/healthcare-data-architecture/">healthcare data architecture</a> and <a href="/regions/">where I work</a>.</p>
<p>If you are bringing AI into healthcare in Kenya or elsewhere in Africa, see my <a href="/ai-architect/">AI architecture work</a>, or <a href="/#assess">analyse your challenge</a> and bring the result to a call.</p>
